# Cloudflare Pages redirects.
#
# Cloudflare caps *dynamic* rules — any rule containing a splat or a
# :placeholder — at 100 per file, and silently drops everything past the cap.
# Static rules are capped at 2100, which is not a constraint here. So a `/*`
# companion is written only for a term page that actually had a subpath.
#
# For a taxonomy term that subpath is only ever pagination, and a term page
# paginated only when it carried more than pagerSize posts. Note that pagerSize
# was 10, not 20, for the whole life of these pages: config.yml carried the
# setting as the dotted key `pagination.PagerSize`, which YAML reads as a
# literal key rather than a nested map, so Hugo never saw it and used its own
# default of 10. Only five retired terms ever cleared that bar, and they are the
# five splats below.
#
# The exception this deliberately gives up on is /page/1/. Hugo emits that
# alias for every term, paginated or not, so each retired term did technically
# have one live subpath. It is not worth a rule: nothing links to it (the
# pagination nav points page 1 at the bare term URL), and it is a meta-refresh
# stub whose canonical is the term page, which the static rule above it already
# redirects. Covering them would need one splat per retired term, which is what
# put this file at 144 dynamic rules and 44 over the cap to begin with.
#
# Category merges, 2026-07: "coding" and "programming" folded into "Dev",
# "introspection-meditation" folded into "Reflection". These term pages were
# live before the merge, so the old URLs are kept pointing at their successors.
# Casing changes (infosec -> InfoSec, etc.) needed no redirects — Hugo slugifies
# terms, so those URLs never changed.

# Category consolidation, 2026-08: the twelve-term set collapsed to four plus a
# catch-all (Cyber, Technology, Reflection, Career, Other). Every retired term
# below was a live page, so each points at whichever category absorbed it. The
# 2026-07 "coding"/"programming" redirects used to land on /categories/dev/,
# which no longer exists — they are retargeted here rather than left to chain
# through a dead page.
#
# "content" goes to /roundup/ because 33 of its 44 posts were the retired
# Lockd & Loaded link roundups; the rest were spread across Technology.
# Individual posts did NOT move — each pins its own `url` — so only these
# taxonomy pages ever needed redirecting.

/categories/infosec                     /categories/cyber/        301
/categories/infosec/*                   /categories/cyber/        301
/categories/privacy                     /categories/cyber/        301
/categories/foss                        /categories/technology/   301
/categories/foss/*                      /categories/technology/   301
/categories/ai                          /categories/technology/   301
/categories/dev                         /categories/technology/   301
/categories/pkm                         /categories/technology/   301
/categories/gaming                      /categories/technology/   301
/categories/family                      /categories/reflection/   301
/categories/content                     /roundup/                 301
/categories/content/*                   /roundup/                 301

/categories/coding                      /categories/technology/   301
/categories/programming                 /categories/technology/   301
/categories/introspection-meditation    /categories/reflection/   301

# Tag consolidation, 2026-08-05: 159 tags down to 30. Each retired tag page
# points at whichever tag absorbed it; the handful with no successor (they
# restated a category, or were one-off proper nouns) go to the tag index.
#
# The six below were not part of that pass and had no successor tag to be
# merged into. They went dead earlier the same day, when the roundup move
# stripped tags from content/roundup/: every post they held was a Lockd &
# Loaded roundup, so removing those tags emptied the terms outright and Hugo
# stopped emitting the pages. They point at /roundup/, the same place
# /categories/content/ goes, because that is where their posts are.
#
# "culture" and "tech" get splats because they were the two largest and did
# paginate. The other four held one post each.

